---
import LetsTalk from '@components/home/LetsTalk.astro'
import Insight from '@components/insights/Insight.astro'
import InsightSignoff from '@components/insights/InsightSignoff.astro'
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro'
import { getInsightNav } from '@data/insights'
import InsightNav from '@components/insights/InsightNav.astro'

const { previous, current, next } = getInsightNav(
  'can-i-build-my-website-myself-with-wix-or-squarespace-or-do-i-need-a-developer'
)
---

<Layout title={current?.title} description={current?.summary}>
  <Insight tags={current?.tags}>
    <span slot="insight-title">{current?.title}</span>
    <span slot="insight-subtitle">{current?.dateLabel}</span>
    <span slot="insight-text">
      <p>
        You would be forgiven for expecting us to say no, always hire a developer, since DIY platforms are direct
        competition for the smaller end of what we do. But the honest answer is that Wix and Squarespace are
        genuinely the right call for a lot of businesses. The trick is knowing which kind of business you actually
        are before you commit to one.
      </p><br />
      <p><strong>DIY works well when:</strong></p><br />
      <ul class="lg:mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>You are still validating the idea.</strong> If the business might look completely different in
            a year, or might not exist at all, spending real money on a custom build does not make sense yet.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>The site is simple and a template genuinely covers it.</strong> A local service business, a personal
            site, an event page. If a template already does what you need, there is no reason to pay for custom work.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>You are willing to actually invest the time.</strong> DIY is not free, it trades money for your
            own hours. That is a fair trade if you have the time and not the budget, but it stops being a saving if
            you do not.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <br />
      <p><strong>It stops being the right call when:</strong></p><br />
      <ul class="lg:mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>You need something the template and its plugin marketplace do not already offer.</strong> Custom
            functionality, unique interactions, integrations with other systems.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>You expect to grow into something more complex within a year or two.</strong> Building on Wix or
            Squarespace now and needing a rebuild later means paying for two websites instead of one.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Platform lock-in matters to you.</strong> These builders are proprietary. Migrating off them later
            is harder than migrating off something like WordPress, you usually cannot cleanly export the site, you
            start again from scratch.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Differentiation matters competitively.</strong> Template constraints tend to produce the same generic
            look and feel across businesses. If your website needs to set you apart from a competitor using the same
            platform, that is a real limitation, not a small one.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>SEO and performance need to hold up at scale.</strong> Fine for a five-page brochure site, more of
            a ceiling once you have a lot of pages or a real product catalog.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <br />
      <p>
        We have had people come to us after outgrowing a DIY site, and people we have told honestly that DIY was still
        the right call for where they are at. Neither answer makes us more money in the short term, but it is the
        honest one.
      </p><br />
      <InsightSignoff />
    </span>
  </Insight>

  <InsightNav previousInsight={previous} nextInsight={next} />

  <LetsTalk config={{ buttons: 'both', primaryCta: 'message' }} />
</Layout>
